%%%%%%Hand 3
https://tinyurl.com/2cencuxd
qx|A3|st||rh||ah|Board 3 IMPs   -   April 24, 2024|pn|hondo717,IraCT,danpsych,aspiring|
md|1SQJ8HTD97432CQ953,SA64HK962DAK65CK6,S97532HJ8DJ8CAJT4,SKTHAQ7543DQTC872|sv|e|
mb|P|mb|1N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with 1N. but West has a really nice 17 HCP with all aces and kings. So if West decided to open 1@D and rebid 2N (showing 18-19) that would be possible. One way to decide whether or not to upgrade marginal hands is to use what I call |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=quicktricks,Quick Trick^*N|CP||FP||at| points.|
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|There are 2 possible ^*BQuick Tricks^*n in each suit for a total of 8 possible ^*BQuick Tricks^*n. There are 40 HCP, so you can think of a ^*BQuick Trick^*n of being equivalent to 5 points (40 divided by 8). So your ^*BQuick Trick Points^*n are your quick trick count multiplied by 5.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If your ^*BQuick Trick Points^*n are equal to or exceed your actual HCP that means you have an above average number of ^*BQuick Tricks^*n. That means you can upgrade your hand and bid more aggressive on marginal decisions.|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West has 4 ^*BQuick Tricks^*n (2 in Diamonds, 1 in Spades, and .5 in hearts and clubs) for 20 ^*BQuick Trick Points^*n. That is more than 17 HCP, meaning the West hand is very upgradable and as I said West can treat the hand as a 2N rebid, although I would also open 1N.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|For more on on the topic  please click the blue Hyperlink here |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=quicktricks,Quick Trick^*N|CP||FP||at|.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|2D|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now let me discuss  the simple |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=superaccepts,Super Accept^*N|CP||FP||at|. If partner transfers and you have 4-card support, you should do what is called a ^*BSuper Accept^*n. Here using that approach, in response to East 2@D transfer West bids 3@H. That is supported by the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| because the 9-card fit means it is okay to bid to the 3 level. |
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|A more sophisticated way to play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=superaccepts,"Super Accepts"^*N|CP||FP||at| is to bid a suit in which you have a doubleton. So if playing sophisticated |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=superaccepts,"Super Accepts"^*N|CP||FP||at|, West should bid 3@C. |mb|3C|
PG| ***** 43 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The answer as to whether or not East should bid 4@D  |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttps://www.bridgehands.com/T/Texas_Transfer.htm,Texas Transfer^*N|CP||FP||at| to 4@H as opposed to 2@D is:
^C A. 2@D followed by 4@H is a mild slam try, whereas 
^C B, bidding 4@D directly is unilateral to bid game (or rarely then bidding RKCB for slam). Let us play that approach here.|
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I was asked whether East is strong enough to at least mildly try for slam. With the 1N opener guarantees a heart fit, let's count East's |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=declarerpoints,Bergen Declarer Points^*N|CP||FP||at| to find out. Of course, you all know that we count the ^*BBergen Declarer Points^*n for the partner with longer trump suit. Here that partner is East, though would not be the Declarer.|
PG| ***** 45 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East hand has 11 HCP, plus 2 length points, less 1 for @DQ10 (although given the 1N opener I might decide not to deduct the 1 point), plus 1 additional point for the 6th heart, and plus 1 for the 2 doubletons for 14. They have at most 32 points since partner has a maximum of 17 or so (maybe 18 with a heart fit) and slam is not likely.|
PG| ***** 46 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So I guess 4@D was the better bid rather than 2@D as a mild slam try. Having said that, let's continue this auction.|
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3S|an|Ace or King|
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|3@S is a slam try and control bid. It denies a diamond control, showing first or second round control in spades, and interest in slam.|
PG| ***** 49 ***** |


mb|P|MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West should now be very interested in slam since he had a maximum NT and has at least a 9 card fit. So West can continue to control bid or bid RKCB. At this table we play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| RKCB meaning when hearts are trump, 4@S rather than 4N is RKCB.|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


mb|4S|

mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5C|an|1 or 4|
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|5@C is 1 or 4--2nd step if playing 0314.|
PG| ***** 52 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|5@D is the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at| and East's response is 5@H with no queen. Here with @HQ, East responds a suit in which she has a king--5@S. |
PG| ***** 53 ***** |


mb|P|mb|5S|an|K|
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|By the way, this hand also illustrates why 4@S as RKCB for hearts is better than 4NT. If 4N were RKCB for hearts then East would respond 5@D, and West would not be able to do the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at|, since 5@H would be to play.|
PG| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|But since 4@S is RKCB, over the second step showing 1 key card, 5@D is available as the ^*BQueen Ask^*n because West is stopping in 5@H missing one key card and the @HQ. That is why West asked for the @HQ.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


mb|P|mb|6H|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 57 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is not clear what North should lead. The @CA is probably wrong, since West bid 4@S RKCB, meaning he must have a club control (the @CK). A heart is possible, although it might give away a trick. A spade is another possibility. Anyway, as you can see this is a very aggressive slam and essentially depends on the @CA being on side.|
PG| ***** 58 ***** |


